  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>MHT-CET PCB career options run through pharmacy, agriculture, biotechnology, and food technology — not through MBBS, nursing, or dentistry, which many students assume by default.</strong>
    CET (short for MHT-CET, Maharashtra's state Common Entrance Test) is a completely different exam from NEET, with a different mark scheme, a different seat pool, and a different admission process. Knowing exactly what it opens and what it does not is the first real decision here.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>MHT-CET PCB career options mainly cover B.Pharm, Pharm.D, agriculture-linked degrees, biotechnology, and food technology inside Maharashtra.</li>
      <li>MHT-CET does not admit you to MBBS, BDS, nursing, or AYUSH courses — those still need NEET or a separate exam.</li>
      <li>The exam itself is 200 marks with no negative marking, a very different risk profile from NEET's 720-mark, negative-marked national test.</li>
      <li>Sitting the exam is only step one — CAP registration and choice filling decide your actual seat.</li>
      <li>The degree that pays off long-term is the one where fit, skill-building, and a real specialisation plan stack up — that combination moves a CET-admitted graduate toward a stronger skill portfolio and earlier financial freedom, not the seat alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Run any CET-admitted course through the 4-Checkpoint Protocol and 3 Gates below before committing years and fees to it.</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you want the wider PCB map first, including MBBS and the full non-MBBS list, read <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th-pcb/">career options after 12th PCB</a>.
    If NEET specifically is not working out for you, <a href="/blog/career-options/pcb-career-options-without-neet/">PCB career options without NEET</a> covers the broader no-NEET map beyond just CET-admitted courses.
  </p>

  <p>
