  <h2 id="outside-state">If you are applying to MHT-CET from outside Maharashtra</h2>

  <p>
    Yes, you can register and sit MHT-CET without Maharashtra domicile.
  </p>

  <p>
    What changes is your seat pool, not your eligibility to attempt the exam.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What stays open to you</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The exam itself — no domicile requirement to register and appear.</li>
        <li>The All India Quota share of seats at participating colleges.</li>
        <li>Any vacant seats released in later CAP rounds after Maharashtra-domicile candidates are allotted.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What does not apply to you</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Maharashtra state-category reservation benefits.</li>
        <li>The EWS quota tied to Maharashtra's own asset and income criteria.</li>
        <li>First-preference priority given to in-state candidates in early CAP rounds.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    If you are not from Maharashtra and pharmacy or agriculture is your genuine goal, treat MHT-CET as one option among several state and private-college entrance routes, not the only door.
  </p>

